godseyj@planetc.com wrote:
>
> I haven't seen this brought up in a while, and it's something that
> still bothers me.

It seems to be discussed about once per year.

> NH seems to arbitrarily treat "Chaotic" as "Evil" or "Not Evil".

False.  The idea is lawfull/neutral/chaotic is taken
from D&D and it is separate from good and evil.  In
some sense Moloch is evil and all of the other deities
are variations on good.  Alignment is about interest
in following the rules.

> Chaotics can murder and sacrafice their own species; these are
> generally considered "evil" things.  However, Elves are Chaotic; Elves
> are generally considered "not evil"*.

Since chaotic means rule-breaking not evil, a chaotic
player is friendly to fellow chaotics.  More folks
breaking the rules is a goal.  If chaotic meant evil
then there would be an alignment bonus for killing
friendlies.

Take the Norse pantheon of the Valk.  All of Tyr, Odin
and Loki are good in the sense they often work on the
same side opposing Surtr who is the opponent.  Tyr is
the diety of the legal system.  Parliments and courts
were consecrated to him, lawfull.  Odin had a mixed
record.  He worked endlessly to delay Ragnarok but he
would do things like cause the weak to win in a duel
to be able to recruit the strong to be one of his
warriors, neutral.  Loki took wild risks and completely
ignored the rules, but his efforts resulted in the
armored walls of Asgard and the creation of Mjollnir,
chaotic.  Still, in the end all were good when
compared with Surtr the fire giant (who is a wimp in
Nethack).  If I were to chose that list it would be
Tyr/lawfull, Thor/neutral, Odin/chaotic as Loki is a
bit over the edge, but that's a matter of tastes.

> At the risk of making NH more similar to D&D, why not implement a more
> cohesive alignment-system - so that Elves, while carefree and Chaotic,
> are restricted from doing specifically Evil things such as murder?

Because that's how it already is implemented.  Killing
a friendly already does hurt alignment and there are
assorted other similar aspects.

